WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.500
The Hulk may be one of Marvel's most famous
heroes, but after the release of The Incredible

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:09.720
Hulk, Bruce Banner was sidelined until The
Avengers rolled around four years later.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.140
And even then, the Hulk's role in the MCU
has largely been a supporting one.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.920
So why did we never get to see The Incredible
Hulk 2?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.400
When The MCU began, their core actors were
in place: Chris Evans as Captain America.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.600
Chris Hemsworth playing Thor.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.180
And of course Robert Downey Jr., nailing the
role of Iron Man.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.200
Well, three out of four isn’t bad, because
we all know what happened with Bruce Banner.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.040
So why did Edward Norton fail to make the
cut for Earth's mightiest heroes?

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.180
While most of the actors playing Avengers
have had a long and prosperous relationship

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.960
with Marvel Studios, Norton seems to have
gotten off on the wrong foot from day one.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:48.500
In fact, he doesn't appear to have even been
the first choice for the role.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.010
In an interview with the Huffington Post,
director Louis Leterrier let it slip that,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.980
while he appreciated what Norton brought to
the table, his first pick had actually been

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.900
Mark Ruffalo.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.900
"I prefer you."

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.540
On top of his runner-up status, numerous rumors
have arisen over the years suggesting that

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.820
Norton may have been difficult to work with
on the set, that he was "very vocal" at times

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.690
when he disagreed with something, and that
he was overly involved in tampering with the

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:14.180
script.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.440
While none of these are particularly rare
qualities in Hollywood stars, they're not

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.940
likely to stack the deck in your favor, either.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.720
It didn't take long after the film came out
for the first hints of discord to begin surfacing.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:30.820
Two years after The Incredible Hulk's release,
Marvel boss Kevin Feige made the announcement

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.520
that Norton would not be brought back to "portray
the title role of Bruce Banner in The Avengers."

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.710
At the time, Feige insisted that the decision
wasn't a monetary one.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:40.500
His statement read:

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.100
"Our decision is definitely not one based
on monetary factors, but instead rooted in

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.690
the need for an actor who embodies the creativity
and collaborative spirit of our other talented

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:50.700
cast members."

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:55.640
Feige's statement made it clear that something
had been going on behind closed doors something

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.420
more than a few friendly disagreements over
the script.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.640
Luckily, the Hulk was still set to appear
in the first Avengers movie and would continue

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.440
to make appearances in other characters' future
films.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.860
But the removal of Norton from the role shed
a little light on why Iron Man, played by

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:15.040
the immensely popular Robert Downey Jr., became
the center of a burgeoning franchise while

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.980
the Hulk languished on the sidelines.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.440
Norton's agent was quick to respond to Feige's
statement.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.560
In his rebuttal, he said:

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:27.020
"This offensive statement from Kevin Feige
at Marvel is a purposefully misleading, inappropriate

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.640
attempt to paint our client in a negative
light."

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.270
The actor himself took to social media, not
to attack Marvel, but rather to express his

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.740
disappointment at the way things had turned
out.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.340
He wrote:

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.790
"I sincerely hoped it could happen and be
great for everyone, but it hasn't turned out

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:42.900
as we all hoped.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.540
I know this is disappointing to many people
and that makes me sad.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.040
But I am very sincerely grateful to Marvel
for extending the offer and even more so for

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.840
giving me the chance to be a part of the Hulk's
long and excellent history."

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.180
These responses from Norton and his agent
seemed to indicate that, no matter how bad

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:03.030
things might have been behind the scenes,
Norton had continued to hope that he would

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:04.040
reprise his role.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.660
Unfortunately, Feige's decision put an end
to those hopes.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.100
As the dust settled, Norton eventually traded
his regretful tune for one of indifference,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.960
explaining a few years later that he had lost
interest in the role, and instead wanted to

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:18.620
act in other movies and genres.

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.411
While it's been rumored for years that Norton
isn't the easiest person to work with, that

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:28.319
doesn't mean the blame for his removal deserves
to fall squarely on his Hulked-out shoulders.

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:32.590
Both Marvel Studios and Disney have developed
a track record over the years for dismissing

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.880
various personnel who haven't fallen in line
with their vision.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.580
For example, Thor: The Dark World's first
director, Patty Jenkins, said she didn't think

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.230
she "could make a great film out of their
script."

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.750
Rather than allowing her to adjust the story,
the studio parted ways with the director…

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:52.400
who went on to head up Wonder Woman, while
Marvel ended up with a subpar sequel to their

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.340
Thor franchise.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:58.680
Terrence Howard also infamously lost the role
of James "Rhodey" Rhodes to Don Cheadle after

00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.360
Iron Man.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.260
And of course, there's Guardians of the Galaxy
franchise mastermind James Gunn, who was fired

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.480
in 2018 after internet users dug up some of
his old and quite offensive tweets.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.940
While Disney may have ultimately reinstated
Gunn to his previous position, the whole Norton

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.140
debacle wasn't the only behind the scenes
misstep Marvel Studios has endured.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:22.830
Although the actual details of the behind-the-scenes
breakup may never be cleared up, however,

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:27.740
one thing that was crystal clear as the door
swung shut on Norton: the Hulk's MCU storyline

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.680
wasn't going anywhere fast.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.300
Rights are a tricky business in Hollywood.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.760
In the case of a standalone Hulk sequel, though,
they're even more nuanced than usual.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.300
While Sony may own the rights to Spider-Man,
for example, the rights to Hulk are more or

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:43.500
less "split" between Marvel and Universal.

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:48.480
But unlike the innovative time-sharing agreement
between Sony and Marvel for Spidey, the Hulk's

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:53.729
situation has been complicated by the less-than-stellar
relationship between the two studios holding

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:54.220
the strings.

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:59.960
According to Forbes, Marvel regained the rights
to the Hulk character in 2005 when Universal

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.930
neglected to pursue a sequel to Ang Lee's
2003 movie Hulk.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.320
"So, they think they can just throw you away
as they did me."

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:13.360
But while that meant Marvel were given the
chance to integrate Hulk into their cinematic

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.300
universe, Universal retained one critical
piece of the puzzle: the "right of first refusal"

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.560
over the distribution of any solo Hulk films.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.900
In other words, while Marvel can use the Hulk
character, if they decide to make a Hulk movie,

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.280
Universal gets first dibs on distributing
the feature.

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:32.479
Of course, Marvel has used other studios to
distribute their movies before, and the lack

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.800
of distribution rights alone isn't enough
of a reason to put the kibosh on a sequel.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.800
But considering all the other issues The Incredible
Hulk faced during production and after its

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.060
release, it can't have helped things much,
either.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.419
It's easy to look at the quarter of a billion
that The Incredible Hulk made in theaters

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:52.600
and dismiss money as a motivation in Marvel's
hesitation to produce a sequel.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.800
But the numbers are actually a bit of an issue.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.980
Even though the movie was released before
the days when Marvel was pumping out billion-dollar

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:03.820
projects a couple of times a year, the film
still underperformed at the box office.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:13.960
During its run, Norton's Hulk brought in $263,427,551
which is a respectable sum, to say the least.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.080
But take Iron Man for comparison.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.580
That movie, which was released just a few
months prior to The Incredible Hulk and which

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.780
cost $10 million less to make brought in twice
as much in global ticket sales.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.199
And while The Incredible Hulk may not have
been a complete flop, its less-than-stellar

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:35.240
box office performance and middling critical
reception likely suggested to Marvel that

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.640
the Hulk alone just isn't compelling enough
to draw a big crowd.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:43.740
Having the Hulk show up for a handful of epic
fight scenes is exactly the kind of integration

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.020
that makes his presence in Marvel's more recent
films worthwhile.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.740
But the truth is, it costs a lot to produce
a whole film centered on a completely CGI

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:56.789
character and that goes doubly when any scene
they're in is going to have to include a kitchen

00:06:56.789 --> 00:07:01.060
sink's worth of flying cars, crumbling buildings,
and all kinds of mayhem.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:06.039
While a huge investment in special effects
is part and parcel of bigger crossover releases

00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:10.879
like the Avengers movies, an entire film of
that nature focused on a single character

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.700
is a daunting task to expect a studio to take
on.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:18.620
And when you take into consideration the disappointing
box office reception of The Incredible Hulk,

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.620
it becomes that much easier to believe that
the Marvel and studio execs involved in the

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.480
decision would hesitate before signing off
on such an expensive project.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:32.139
Although the snowball effect of subpar ticket
sales, uninspired reviews, production costs,

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:36.029
behind the scenes drama, and distribution
rights would have already been more than enough

00:07:36.029 --> 00:07:40.840
to put the final nail in the coffin of any
Hulk sequel, that's not the end of the story.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.210
In many ways, the true Achilles heel of the
Hulk's one and only MCU outing was that it

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:49.300
simply failed to fit the tone of the wider
MCU.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:54.000
Whether it was the solemn introspection that
Norton tried to bring to the role or the inherent

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.639
difficulties in exploring the Hulk's mindless
destruction as the centerpiece of a story,

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:02.620
The Incredible Hulk ended up struggling to
hit the effortlessly entertaining tone of

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.880
most of the following MCU films.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:10.080
Taking place in Phase One of the MCU, Hulk's
film was set against characters like the selfishly

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:16.080
sarcastic Tony Stark, the lovably loyal Steve
Rogers and the hilariously hunky Thor all

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.300
of whom were busy setting the stage for the
first Avengers movie.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:24.779
In comparison, Hulk's more serious overtones
stood out like a big, green sore thumb and

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.300
made the film dark enough to dampen the enthusiasm
of even the most ardent Marvel fan.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:33.940
As if the sobering tone of the film wasn't
enough, there's also the fact that the story

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:38.860
started by dropping the audience into a random
spot in Banner and the Hulk's story arc and

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.560
then proceeded to take it absolutely nowhere.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.620
After skipping over the famous gamma radiation
exposure incident, the film proceeded to meander

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.140
awkwardly through Banner's harrowingly unheroic
adventures.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:54.400
He finally flashed a little superhero muscle
when he subdued Abomination in the streets

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.440
of Harlem, but when the film ended shortly
after things once again took a step backward.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:03.400
Banner's takeaway from the experience was
simply that he had found a way to come to

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.880
terms with the Other Guy.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:08.750
And while this appeared to be a positive step
forward for the character, the fact that it

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:13.540
was quickly retconned when Ruffalo's distressed
Banner was brought back on the scene in The

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.820
Avengers made the whole point of the film
pretty much... pointless.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:23.020
In July 2017, Mark Ruffalo predicted that,
quote, "a standalone Hulk movie will never

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:23.560
happen."

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:28.060
And while that may have seemed like the end
of the story, you could argue that a secret

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:30.860
Incredible Hulk follow-up already exists.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:33.880
"Hulk!

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.080
Hulk!

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.700
Hulk!"

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.360
Around the same time that Ruffalo dashed fans'
hopes of ever seeing another solo Hulk film,

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.710
the actor also revealed in another interview
that he'd sat down with Kevin Feige and the

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:49.980
two had brainstormed what a standalone film
for the Hulk would look like.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:55.220
Once they had a good idea to work from, they
decided to implement it into the wider MCU

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.630
by divvying up the story across three separate
movies.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:02.710
Cleverly, none of these would be released
as a standalone film, thus sidestepping the

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:04.300
rights issue with Universal.

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.700
Instead, the Hulk storyline would be woven
through Thor: Ragnarok, Avengers: Infinity

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:13.600
War, and Avengers: Endgame, during which his
"sequel storyline" could finally be told one

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:14.780
piece at a time.

